## 4. Phase-by-Phase Technical Deep Dive

### Phase 1 — Data Loading & EDA

**Feature Engineering on DataCo:**
```python
df['delivery_delay']  = df['Days for shipment (scheduled)'] - df['Days for shipping (real)']
df['is_late']         = (df['delivery_delay'] < 0).astype(int)
df['profit_margin']   = df['Benefit per order'] / (df['Sales'] + 1e-9)
```

**Time-Series Features on Demand:**
```python
for lag in [7, 14, 28]:
    demand_grouped[f'lag_{lag}'] = demand_grouped.groupby(
        ['Warehouse', 'Product_Code'])['Order_Demand'].shift(lag)
demand_grouped['rolling_mean_7'] = ...transform(lambda x: x.rolling(7).mean())
demand_grouped['rolling_std_7']  = ...transform(lambda x: x.rolling(7).std())
```

**Outputs:** `dataco_clean.parquet` (180,519 × 58), `demand_grouped.parquet` (634,553 × 9)

---

### Phase 2a — SARIMA Baseline

**Model:** `SARIMAX(1,1,1)(1,1,1,52)` — captures weekly seasonality at period 52 (annual)

**Stationarity test:** Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF). If p-value > 0.05 → first-order differencing applied.

**Train/Test:** 80/20 chronological split on the top-selling product (Product_1359, 262 weekly observations)

**Results (actual run):**
| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| MAE | 599,512.8 |
| RMSE | 740,451.7 |
| MAPE | 86.0% |

> SARIMA performs poorly on this data — high MAPE indicates high demand volatility and multi-SKU patterns that SARIMA can't capture with a single univariate model.

---

### Phase 2b — XGBoost + Optuna HPO

**Feature set (12 features):**
```
warehouse_enc, product_enc, month, quarter, year, dayofyear, week,
lag_7, lag_14, lag_28, rolling_mean_7, rolling_std_7
```

**Hyperparameter search:** Optuna TPE sampler, 30 trials, minimising MAE on hold-out set

**Best params found:**
```python
{max_depth: 5, lr: ~0.17, n_est: 152, sub: 0.926, col: 0.953}
```

**Train/Test split:** Chronological cutoff at 2016-01-01 (438,776 train / 118,211 test records)

**Results (actual run):**
| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Best Optuna MAE | 6,305.4 |
| Final MAE | 6,355.5 |
| RMSE | 33,163.3 |
| MAPE | 6,120.2% |

> High MAPE is misleading here — it's driven by near-zero demand periods in the denominator. The absolute MAE of ~6,355 units is the more informative metric.

**Saved:** `src/models/xgb_demand.pkl`

---

### Phase 2c — LSTM (PyTorch)

**Architecture:**
```
Input (seq_len=30, 1 feature)
  → LSTM(128 hidden, 2 layers, dropout=0.2, batch_first=True)
  → LayerNorm(128)
  → Linear(128 → 64) → ReLU → Dropout(0.2)
  → Linear(64 → 7)          ← 7-day forecast output
```

**Training setup:**
- Loss: `HuberLoss` (robust to outliers vs MSE)
- Optimizer: Adam (lr=1e-3)
- Scheduler: `ReduceLROnPlateau(patience=5)`
- Gradient clipping: norm=1.0

**Data:** Product_1359 weekly time series → MinMaxScaled → 30-step sliding windows

**Epoch losses (actual run):**
| Epoch | Loss |
|---|---|
| 10 | 0.0268 |
| 20 | 0.0245 |
| 30 | 0.0253 |
| 40 | 0.0251 |
| 50 | 0.0249 |

**Results (actual run):**
| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| MAE | 521,884.9 – 538,130.0 |
| RMSE | 633,497.8 – 644,614.8 |
| MAPE | 49.6% – 52.3% |

> LSTM significantly outperforms SARIMA (MAPE ~50% vs 86%) and is the chosen inference model for the API. The large absolute errors reflect the raw demand scale (up to millions of units/week for top SKUs).

**Saved:** `src/models/lstm_demand.pt`, `src/models/lstm_scaler.pkl`

---

### Phase 3 — Vehicle Routing Optimisation (VRP)

**Problem formulation:** Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem (relaxed — no capacity constraints, minimise total distance)

**Algorithm:** Greedy Nearest-Neighbour Heuristic
- Start all vehicles at depot (centroid of all stops)
- Each step: assign the closest unvisited stop to the current vehicle, round-robin across vehicles
- Return each vehicle to depot at end

**Key performance fix:**
```python
# BEFORE (broken - O(n²) pure Python):
best_idx = min(unvisited,
    key=lambda i: haversine(cur_lat[v], cur_lng[v],
                            sample.loc[i, 'lat'], sample.loc[i, 'lng']))

# AFTER (fixed - vectorized NumPy distance matrix pre-computed once):
dist_matrix = haversine_matrix(all_lats, all_lons)  # (201×201), computed once
dists = dist_matrix[cur_node[v], uv_list]           # O(1) lookup
best_node = uv_list[np.argmin(dists)]               # instant argmin
```
Runtime improvement: **minutes → ~7 seconds**

**Results (actual run):**
| Vehicle | Stops | Distance (km) |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 40 | 6,454.0 |
| 2 | 40 | 9,275.3 |
| 3 | 40 | 7,936.0 |
| 4 | 40 | 7,740.6 |
| 5 | 40 | 5,019.7 |
| **Total** | **200** | **36,425.7 km** |

**Route Map:**

![Greedy Nearest-Neighbour VRP - Brazil Deliveries](C:\Users\shubh\.gemini\antigravity\brain\82a90f1c-2153-4485-88c7-716de0971084\route_optimization.png)

**Saved:** `reports/route_summary.csv`, `reports/route_optimization.png`

